.TH SCHED 9
.SH NAME
sched \- threads, priorities, the tick, placement across cores
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "kernel:sched"
.PP
.nf
.B "spawn :: proc (name: string, entry: Thread_Proc, arg: rawptr = nil, priority: Priority = PRIORITY_NORMAL, affinity: Cpu_Classes = ANY_CLASS, stack_size: int = DEFAULT_STACK_SIZE, space: ^mem.Address_Space = nil) -> ^Thread"
.B "spawn_user :: proc (name: string, space: ^mem.Address_Space, entry, sp: uintptr, arg0, arg1, arg2: u64 = 0, record: rawptr = nil, priority: Priority = PRIORITY_NORMAL, stack_size: int = DEFAULT_STACK_SIZE) -> ^Thread"
.B "current :: proc () -> ^Thread"
.B "cpu :: proc () -> ^Cpu"
.B "block :: proc (pending: ^rawptr)"
.B "ready :: proc (t: ^Thread)"
.B "unpark :: proc (t: ^Thread)"
.B "yield :: proc ()"
.B "exit :: proc () -> !"
.B "reap :: proc ()"
.B "kill_current :: proc (r: arch.Resume) -> arch.Resume"
.B "park_current :: proc (r: arch.Resume) -> arch.Resume"
.B "reschedule :: proc (r: arch.Resume, spent_slice: bool) -> arch.Resume"
.B "stats :: proc () -> Stats"
.B "cpu_stats :: proc (id: int) -> Cpu_Stats"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sched
is the kernel thread scheduler.
One entry point into a context switch,
.BR reschedule ,
and one way out of it: a voluntary
.B yield
and a timer preemption both arrive as an interrupt and both call it,
returning a different
.B arch.Resume
to the trap tail that resumes whichever thread was chosen.
.SS Thread
A
.B Thread
has one stack, one saved
.B arch.Resume
and, for a thread running a program rather than kernel code, an
.B ^mem.Address_Space
in its
.B space
field.
A kernel thread carries nil there, which is what makes a switch between
two kernel threads cost one pointer comparison and no page table reload.
Nothing in this package treats a thread running ring 3 code specially:
the privilege level lives in the saved frame, not in the
.BR Thread .
.B Thread_State
is one of
.BR Ready ,
.BR Running ,
.B Blocked
or
.BR Dead .
.SS Priority and decay
Sixteen levels,
.B PRIORITY_IDLE
at 0 through
.B PRIORITY_MAX
at 15.
Level 0 belongs only to the idle thread.
.B PRIORITY_REALTIME
is 13; nothing above it decays, and nothing in Vectra runs there yet.
.B PRIORITY_NORMAL
is 8, where a new thread starts.
A thread has a
.B base
priority, set at creation, and a
.B prio
it actually runs at.
.I Decay
drops
.B prio
one level each time the thread consumes a whole slice of CPU without
blocking, floored at
.BR PRIORITY_MIN .
.I Boost
raises a woken thread back to its
.B base
plus one, capped below realtime.
That is the whole anti-starvation mechanism: a compute-bound thread
settles near the bottom of the range within a few slices, and an
interactive one stays near the top, with nothing telling either which it
is.
.B ready
boosts the thread it wakes;
.B unpark
does not, because a thread woken only because a lock came free waited on
nothing outside itself and a boost there would pay it for contention.
.SS Slice and tick
.B QUANTUM_TICKS
is ten ticks at the scheduler's 1 kHz default, the slice a thread gets on
a full-capacity core.
.B slice_ticks
scales that by a core's capacity, so a thread moved to a slower core gets
proportionally more ticks rather than proportionally less work.
.B ticks_left
is refilled only when it reaches zero, never on every dispatch: a thread
that blocks keeps what is left of its slice and resumes on it, so the
slice measures CPU actually consumed rather than time between blocks.
.SS spawn, block, ready
.I Spawn
creates a kernel thread, chooses a core by
.B pick_cpu
and queues it there.
.I Spawn_user
creates a thread whose first instruction is in ring 3: the same
construction, differing only in the selectors
.B arch.thread_user_init
writes into the frame.
.I Block
takes the calling thread off every queue until something calls
.B ready
or
.B unpark
on it, unless the wait it is parking for is already satisfied; the
scheduler lock is held across the switch itself, released only once the
incoming thread's frame is written and nothing on this core can read the
blocked thread's stack again.
.I Yield
raises the same software interrupt a preemption would, so a voluntary
switch and a timer switch share one path; a yielding thread does not
decay, because it did not burn its slice.
.I Exit
marks the calling thread dead and does not return; the next
.I spawn
on that core calls
.I reap
to give its stack back, because a dead thread's stack cannot be freed
until something else is running on it.
.B kill_current
does the same from a fault handler, on the interrupted thread's own
.BR arch.Resume .
.SS Placement and core classes
.B Cpu_Classes
is a set of
.BR arch.Cpu_Class :
.BR Efficiency ,
.B Performance
and
.BR Prime .
On amd64 every core is
.BR .Performance ;
the vocabulary exists for a port with more than one class.
An empty
.B Cpu_Classes
means no constraint, not the empty set.
.B pick_cpu
chooses the least-loaded core, by ready-queue length scaled to the core's
capacity, among the classes a thread's affinity allows, and falls back to
the boot core if no online core satisfies the affinity.
A wake is placed afresh by the same search rather than returned to the
core it last ran on, since
.I pick_cpu
may find it a better home; an idle core chosen this way is kicked with
.B VECTOR_WAKE
so it leaves its halt immediately rather than at its next tick.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/sched
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sync (9),
.IR arch (9),
.IR smp (9),
.IR space (9)
.SH BUGS
There is no realtime thread in the tree; the level exists and is never
used.
There is no cross-core load balancing once a thread is placed. It moves
only when it blocks and wakes.
